          %title Contents
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#existential_graphs"} Existential graphs
              %li
                %a{:href => "#deduction_induction_abduction"} Deduction, induction, abduction
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %p
            Charles Sanders Peirce (1839-1914), a philosopher, logician and scientist, was one of the founders of modern symbolic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            = succeed "." do
              %em pragmaticism
          %p
            Peirce was a prolific thinker who left behind an enormous corpus of work, much unpublished. Where his
            %em Collected Papers
            run to 8 volumes, an ongoing edition aims to publish 30 volumes.
          %p Peirce’s philosophy may be seen as Schellingism transformed in light of (in Peirce’s time) modern physics, as Peirce himself notes in an 1894 letter to William James:
          %blockquote
            %p
              My views were probably influenced by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schelling"} Schelling
              \- by all stages of Schelling, but especially the
              = succeed "." do
                %em Philosophie der Natur
          %h2#existential_graphs Existential graphs
          %p
            Peirce devised a graphical notation, known as
            = succeed "," do
              %strong existential graphs
            = succeed "," do
              %em alpha
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
            = succeed "," do
              %em beta
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            = succeed "," do
              %em gamma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            (
            %a{:href => "#MaPiet18"}> MaPiet 18
            ).
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geraldine+Brady"} Geraldine Brady
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
            have given a category theoretic interpretation of the alpha and beta systems. The latter, a form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagrammatic
            notation, was developed (
            %a{:href => "#PontoShul"}> PontoShul
            ) into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            notation for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+monoidal+categories"} indexed monoidal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MellZeil"} MellZeil
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BSS18"} BSS18
          %h2#deduction_induction_abduction Deduction, induction, abduction
          %p
            In a series of
            %a{:href => "#Peirce98"} lectures
            by Peirce on reasoning, he gives one of those triads he loves so much concerning different modes of inference:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+logic"} deduction
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+logic"} induction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abductive+reasoning"} abduction
            (or retroduction), as filling in different parts of a syllogism. Given logical relations between three concepts,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Deduction strings together, say,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                to give
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              %p
                Induction looks to generalise from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                as a sample of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              %p
                Abduction looks to explain why
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p Peirce gives related examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Deduction
              %ul
                %li All beans in that bag are white.
                %li These beans are from that bag.
                %li Therefore, these beans are white.
            %li
              %p
                %strong Induction
              %ul
                %li These beans are from that bag.
                %li These beans are white.
                %li Therefore, all beans in that bag are white.
            %li
              %p
                %strong Abduction
              %ul
                %li These beans are white.
                %li All beans in that bag are white.
                %li Therefore, these beans are from that bag.
          %p
            Seen from the point of view of category theory, these would seem to resemble:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension"} extension
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting"} lifting
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Corf25"} Corf 25
          %h2#references References
          %p Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entries:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/peirce/"} Charles Sanders Peirce
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/peirce-logic/"} Peirce’s Deductive Logic
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/peirce-semiotics/"} Peirce’s Theory of Signs
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/peirce/self-contextualization.html"} Peirce’s View of the Relationship Between His Own Work and German Idealism
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/abduction/peirce.html"} Peirce on Abduction
          %p Charles S. Peirce studies
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.peirce.org/"} website
